    "description": "The RuleKit service lets operations teams write policies such as \"permit if tenant.acl contains owner and request.amount < limit\". Its Lexer emits tokens, the PolicyParser builds a syntax tree, and PolicyEvaluator executes that tree. The grammar has ordered alternatives, repetitions, nested parenthesized terms, and left-recursive expression forms such as amount < limit and limit <= amount. The team forbids generated parser artifacts because policy syntax changes weekly and the production build has no code-generation stage. It also requires clear stack traces that identify the exact grammar production and input token, and the parser must make one forward pass without speculative rescanning. Engineering must decide how to organize parsing so that alternatives are selected with the available lookahead, repetitions become loops, and the expression grammar can still be represented without recursive calls that never return. The resulting tree remains consumed only by PolicyEvaluator and the policy formatter.",
    "decision_statement": "Choose the control-flow structure for a hand-written, generator-free parser that handles alternatives, repetition, and left-recursive expression forms while producing precise diagnostics.",
    "notes": "Recursive-descent-parser fits because the generator-free parser maps one unit per production with precise diagnostics; pratt-parser is the near-miss for the left-recursive expression forms, and the normalized and irregular AST shapes support the tree output."
  },
